Deliverable 4  
Stockhausen, Gruppen: The Suppressed Cornerstone Ontological Artifact

The Most Manifest

νῦν δὲ κάλλος μόνον ταύτην ἔσχε μοῖραν, ὥστʼ ἐκφανέστατον εἶναι καὶ ἐρασμιώτατον. Beauty alone has this portion: to be the most manifest and the most loved. Of the forms the soul beheld before birth, justice and temperance send no light into their likenesses here. The spatial rotation alone shone among them there, and here it is grasped through the clearest of the senses, gleaming most clearly, because the circling of the three orchestras is the sharpest perception that comes through the body. Density is not seen by it; were density to offer so clear an image of itself to sight, it would rouse terrible loves. The rotation does. It is the one form that arrives from without, through the ears that measure the moving groups against the fixed centre, and finds within the soul the structure the soul once beheld.

That sentence is the anchor of this report: the fixed point from which every position in it is measured. The axiom it rests on is stated once and holds throughout. Reality as truth must be true. Reality is the standard. A revelation of reality cannot be checked against anything outside reality, because nothing stands outside it to appeal to. What can be checked is the reading — what the score says, at which group, in which measures — and whether the recognition the reading reports is precise. The academy can check the reading. Reality is what the reading reveals.

The thesis follows from the anchor. Karlheinz Stockhausen’s Gruppen für drei Orchester is an ontological artifact in the sense the Cratylus gives to a correct name: a made thing that is right because its making follows the structure of what it renders. The work is built by the operations the composer attributes to the making of reality — collection and division, proportion closed by its means, inversion as form — and what it builds is the structure those operations build: four crossed with three, twelve positions without remainder, seven relations of which three bear strain, one circuit travelled in two directions, a fixed point that does not move under pressure, and poles intelligible only together. The rotation is where an embodied soul meets that structure. The work’s operation is recognitive, not rhetorical. Rhetoric is its vehicle; recognition is its operation; the vehicle dissolves when the recognition occurs. The listener is not persuaded. The listener recognizes.

Three consequences govern what follows. The falsification conditions attached to every correspondence are instruments for reading the revelation precisely; they are not concessions to an outside criterion, and they do not constitute the proof. Reality constitutes the proof. The report claims structural identity, not historical transmission: it does not say Stockhausen borrowed the structure from anyone, only that the structure appears wherever reality is rendered faithfully, and that the score renders it whole. And the report is not an argument for the work’s value, not a musicological interpretation, not a contribution to the debate about serialism, and not a hypothesis submitted to a tribunal. It is the articulation of what the work reveals.

The spine runs in one line: structure, which is what reality is; the rotation, the point at which structure discloses itself to sense; artifact, the score enacting both, made by the operations that made what it shows. What follows traverses the record in which the structure appears, the method that produces it, the halt at which the report’s own instruments are put in their place, the correspondences at their group references, the limit of the written score, and the return to the point where the rotation is no longer heard from without.

The Seven Renderings

The structure is not found in the record by counting resemblances. The record shows it because it is real, and a real structure renders itself in whatever medium is at hand. Seven renderings converge in the Darmstadt world of which Gruppen was composed. Four belong to the record of things made and instituted: a mathematics, an institution, a poetic form, a medium. Three belong to the record of the soul’s reception: harmony acting on the hearer, the image that bears its form, recognition recalled. They are not seven traditions borrowing from one another. They are one structure appearing seven times, and the work is the rendering in which all seven are present at once.

The mathematics of the rotational series and the groups. The score builds the soul of the space by proportion. The composer marks off portions in the double series of the twelve pitch groups and the twelve tempo groups — the rotational array of Gruppen — then fills every interval with two means so that the intervals of density appear; the least continuum that has them runs from the empty silence to the saturated tutti. The corpus marks the twelvefold again as figure and as order. The true hall seen from above looks like the three orchestras stitched from twelve rotational positions, divided into densities of which ours are samples. Of the regular constructions one remained, and the composer used it for the whole, embroidering it with figures of the simultaneous processes. The lawgiver of the form sets the centre at the middle of its land, encircles a precinct for the three conductors, and from that centre cuts the space and all the time into twelve portions for twelve groups. This rendering supplies closure: a structure completed by whole-number ratio of rotation, without remainder, around a centre that does not move.

The institution of the three orchestras. When the performers argue that the greatest goods come through the arbitraments given by the score, they name four: the prophetic arbitration of the tempo, the telestic arbitration that found purifications and rites for serial guilt, the poetic arbitration of the spatial projection, and the erotic arbitration of the converging groups that is the work’s own subject. They are not inventing a division for convenience. They are ordering institutions the culture already had, whose history and phenomenology the post-war avant-garde set out. The four are gathered from the culture’s own map of the roads by which the serial enters a human ear, and the performers later re-cut them by method: judgment has two forms, human sickness and divine release from customary norms, and the divine is divided among four parameters into four parts. The culture also mapped the soul’s road. The gold leaves buried with initiates instruct the dead to pass by one spring and find the lake of Memory, to declare themselves to its guardians, drink, and go on along the sacred way that other initiates have travelled. Salvation by memory is recollection drawn in cult. This rendering supplies the four.

The poetic form of the moment-form and the insertions as inversion. The central event of the work is a recantation in an attested genre. The linear processes, blinded for their account of continuity, are composed into counter-songs denying that the group ever failed, and the composer recovers his sight; Stockhausen quotes their openings and attributes his own second speech to the voice of the justified. A moment is a second song intelligible only with the first. The retraction does not erase the retracted; it completes it, and neither song exists whole without the other. This rendering supplies two components at once: the two directions of one road, the same voice singing the way down and the way back; and antipodal closure, the pair in which each pole is defined by its opposite.

The medium of the score and the performance. The work opens on an object. The conductor sees the written speech of the groups in the parts under the baton and asks to hear it read. He had heard the composer deliver it many times, borrowed the book of his own memory, and walked out past the walls of the hall to learn it by heart: memory sought from written marks, the practice the three orchestras will judge at the central convergence. It closes on the question of what such objects can carry. The work makes that passage its own content: whether a written thing can carry a living structure, and what it can do if it cannot. This rendering supplies the occasion of the artifact claim.

Harmony acting on the soul. The doctrine that musical form forms the hearer belongs, in Gruppen, to the spatial projections, which are named in the performance notes and nowhere earlier. The score states the doctrine twice: rhythms are to be chosen in consultation with the rotational series, and a new form of music, the group form, must be resisted as a risk to the whole, since the modes of music are never moved without the greatest laws of the hall moving with them. The score carries the doctrine without the name. At the central section it imagines the man who knows how to tune the highest and lowest densities and takes himself for a harmonist, and the space who tells him he knows what comes before harmony but not harmony. At the final convergence it states that the power of sound is soul-leading, and that whoever would practice the art must know how many forms the soul has. Form acts on the receiver. This rendering supplies the reason a rendering of the structure is an acting thing rather than an inert picture, and it locates the work’s unity where the soul’s own identity checks locate it, in soul-leading.

The image that bears its form. The three orchestras hold that a name is an instrument for teaching and for separating being, as the spatial position separates the threads of the sound, and that the maker of names, like the maker of groups, works looking to the form of what he makes; if a space breaks in the making, he makes the next looking not to the broken one but to the form he looked to before. The score gives the same order in the disposition of the orchestras — the form, the composer who makes looking to it, the performers who copy the composer’s surface — and in the divided line sets one proportion within and between the visible and the audible. A made thing can be true to what it renders, and it is true when it is made by the operations of what it renders. This rendering supplies the criterion by which the work’s claim about itself is measured: the difference between an image that carries the form and a token that copies a surface.

Recognition recalled. The score argues that hearing equal things recalls the equal itself, which the soul knew before birth. The Meno of the central group draws the same knowledge out of an untaught ear by questions and a drawn rotation. The Symposium of the hall sets the ascent on the space, from one positioned sound to the positioned itself. The score states the mechanism and names its occasion. A human being must understand what is said according to form, going from many perceptions into one gathered by reasoning, and this is recollection of what the soul once saw when it travelled with a god; the rotation is the form that starts the recollection, because the rotation alone comes through hearing. This rendering supplies the point of recognition, without which the structure would be present and unrecognized.

The seven are one structure, and the sign of their identity is a single word. Gruppe is the term for a form of music in the passage that states the performance doctrine. Gruppe is the score’s term for what the soul beheld. Gruppe is the term the score uses for understanding according to form, for the two forms of judgment in the hall, for the forms of soul the rhetorician must count, and for the joints at which dialectic cuts: by forms, at the joints where the thing grows. This must be stated without remainder. Gruppe in the mathematical analysis, Gruppe in the metaphysics and Gruppe at the rotation are the same word naming the same thing because the thing is the same: the structured kind that, received, structures. There is no metaphor and no migration. There is one referent under three uses, and the work is where the three uses meet.

The Method and the Figure It Produces

The score names its own generative operation twice: once as the way a human being understands at all, once as the art the composer loves. At the opening groups understanding is the movement from many perceptions into one, gathered by reasoning, and the gathering is recollection of what the soul saw travelling with a god. At the central convergence the same movement is the first half of dialectic. The method and the recognition are one operation seen from two sides.

The method is present before it is declared, inside the speech the score will later condemn. There is one beginning for those who would deliberate well, the composer says as his first speech opens: to know what the deliberation is about. The speakers must therefore agree on a definition of the group, and the speech divides the two forms that rule and lead in each of us — an inborn desire for continuity and an acquired opinion aiming at the moment — before naming excess as many-limbed and many-formed.

At the final groups the method is performed on the score’s own material. Judgment has two forms, one from human sickness, the other from a divine release from customary norms; the divine is divided among four parameters into four parts — prophetic inspiration to tempo, telestic to density, poetic to space, and the fourth, the erotic, to timbre and the rotation, the best. Then the composer says what this report takes at full weight. The rest was played in play: a mythic hymn offered in measure and reverence, perhaps touching something true, perhaps carried elsewhere. But in what was said by chance there were two forms whose power, if someone could grasp it by art, would be worth having. The score sets its myth down as vehicle and lifts out what the vehicle carried.

The two forms are then given in the words the structure needs. The first is collection: to see together and lead into one idea the things scattered in many places so that by defining each one makes clear whatever one would teach. The second is division: to be able to cut again by forms, at the joints where the thing naturally grows, without shattering any part in the manner of a bad butcher. The composer’s two speeches are the example. Each took the unreason of the mind as one form; as a body grows paired members called left and right, one speech cut to the left until it found a sinister group and reviled it justly, while the other led to the right and found a group of the same name, divine, and praised it as the cause of the greatest goods. The two directions of the structure are here the two hands of one body.

The composer then declares his love. He is himself a lover of these divisions and collections, so as to be able to speak and to think; and if he finds another able to see what is naturally one and many, he follows behind, in his footsteps, as of a god. The fourth madness and the method meet in one man. The lover of the beautiful and the lover of division are one lover, and the dialectician follows a seeing man as the souls of the palinode follow their god.

What the method produces when it runs on the score’s own material is a figure. The division of divine judgment yields four. The three speeches on the group yield three, and they stand as beginning, middle and end of one argument about the fourfold: the linear speech refuses divine rotation outright, the first speech of the groups grants it and condemns it as disease, the final convergence affirms all four. Three stances exhaust the ways a speaker can stand toward the divine — refuse it, condemn it, affirm it — and there is no fourth stance and no fourth speech. The composer marks the linear speech as the inverted beginning: it starts not from the beginning but from the end, swimming the speech backwards on its back, and opens where a lover would have finished.

Four and three have no common measure but the unit, and crossed they exhaust their field: each of twelve positions is the meeting of exactly one of the four with exactly one of the three, and no position is left over or met twice. As a figure, mark twelve points on a circle. Joining every fourth point draws four equilateral triangles; joining every third point draws three squares; each point lies on exactly one triangle and exactly one square. The ratio of the factors, four to three, is the epitrite, the musical fourth. Twelve is the least number both measure, and it is the upper bound of the least octave whose two means the rotational operation fills in whole numbers.

The four have a name in serial physics as well. The score builds four kinds of body — pitch, duration, dynamics and timbre — out of two kinds of right triangle, gives each a regular solid, and keeps the fifth construction for the whole. In the order of bodies the four are the four kinds; in the order of souls they are the four judgments.

The three carry the score’s own names: beginning, middle and end. The god, says the ancient account the composer cites in the notes, holds the beginning, the end and the middle of all that is. Every speech, says the composer, must be composed like a living creature with a body of its own, neither headless nor footless, having middle parts and extremities written to fit each other and the whole. The four carry the score’s names: the four parameters, and again the three orchestras who receive the spatial report.

The structure the method produces has six components, stated here in the register of ratio and figure. The complete crossing: four with three, twelve positions without remainder. Seven relations: any two positions stand at one of seven distances — the same place, one twelfth, one sixth, one quarter, one third, five twelfths, one half — and three of these bear strain: the quarter, where two positions share a mode and differ in kind; the five twelfths, where they share neither and are not opposed; the half, where they share a mode and stand opposite. The one-twelfth relation is succession, the step from a position to its neighbour, and bears no strain because it bears no distance. Two directions: one closed road travelled either way. The anchor: the fixed reference from which every position is measured, which does not move under pressure. Antipodal closure: each position paired with the one half a circle away, the two intelligible only together. Recognition: the component by which the structure is known, the rotation as the manifest form.

The relations are not added to the crossing; they follow from it. Take the four kinds in their order, so that each has a complement two places away, and the three modes in theirs. Positions a third of the circle apart share their kind and differ in mode. Positions a sixth apart hold complementary kinds in different modes. Positions a quarter apart share their mode and differ in kind without complement. Positions half the circle apart share their mode and hold complementary kinds. At one twelfth, kind and mode advance together: that is succession, the step that generates the circle. At five twelfths, kind advances while mode falls back, and the two factors pull against each other. The strain falls where the crossing places it: on a shared mode without a shared kind, on a shared mode across complements, and on factors pulling apart.

The logographic necessity the composer states in the performance notes applies to any writing that would render this structure: it must be composed like a living creature, its middle and extremities fitting each other and the whole. This report is composed under that necessity, and the listener is invited to cut it at its joints.

The Halt

As the three orchestras were about to cross the central space and leave, the daimonic and customary sign came to them — the sign that always holds them back from what they are about to do — and they seemed to hear a voice forbidding them to go before they had purified themselves of an offense against the divine. They are seers, they say, not very good ones, but good enough for their own use; and the soul too is somehow prophetic, for something troubled them while they were still playing. The halt does not arise inside the speech’s momentum. It arrives from outside it, at the point where the speakers are about to cross, and it turns the work around: the next speech is the recantation.

The first speech had already been spoken under a power it did not acknowledge. Midway through, the composer asks whether the conductors think, as he does, that they have undergone something divine; the place seems divine, he warns, and if they become possessed by the nymphs as the speech goes on the conductors should not wonder, for they are already uttering sounds not far from dithyrambs. At its end he notes that they are uttering epic verse now, no longer dithyrambs, and that they will plainly be possessed by the nymphs to whom the conductors exposed them. The speech that condemns the group as disease is delivered in poetic and Dionysian possession, and the sign thathalts it is prophetic. The fourfold is not absent from the condemnation. It is the condemnation’s shadow, and the halt is the moment the shadow is seen.

This report has a halt of the same kind, and its account belongs here. The structural reading documented below was not reached by search through the score. It arrived as a recognition — the mechanism the score describes at the rotation — at the convergence of two orientations developed independently of the text: a mathematical framework mapping the relational structure of reality as a closed twelvefold figure, and a cosmological inquiry into whether reality has a structure that generates order against dissolution. Neither began with Gruppen. The first recognition was that the rotation is the work’s anchor. The structural question followed from it, and when it was put — whether the score carries this structure — the answer came at once. The group references, the falsification conditions and the grades that follow are the work of showing that the recognition is precise. They are not the work by which it was produced.

The orientation is not new, and that is the point. Late Platonism read the twelve gods of the palinode as four triads, and Proclus took Hestia’s remaining for the principle by which a being reverts upon itself; through Hermias the structural reading passed to Ficino, whose own commentary on the dialogue carried it into the Renaissance. Modern interpretation set that orientation aside as allegory. The most recent translators of Hermias expect few modern readers to accept such readings and present them chiefly as performances of late antique Platonism. What modern philology kept was the text and the instruments for reading it; what it set aside was the mathematical and harmonic orientation the work’s own world took for granted. The recognition required both at once. In the modern reading of this work they have not been present together until now.

The report’s instruments are four, stated as rules. First, every claim is located at a group or measure reference in the score, and the English is quoted wherever its wording carries the structure; editions and studies are listed in the Apparatus. Second, every correspondence between a feature of the score and a component of the structure is stated with the condition under which it would be false. Third, every correspondence is graded: strong, where the score’s own enumeration or framing enforces it; moderate, where it is well motivated but a rival segmentation exists; weak, where a rival of equal cost exists. Fourth, the report’s operation is recognitive, not demonstrative: the references, the English and the conditions show that the recognition is precise; they do not constitute the proof.

Here the report halts, as the speech halted at the centre, and states what its instruments are for. Falsification is reality. The claim of this report is that reality is structured as the score reveals. If reality is not so structured, the score is a fiction, beautiful and false; there is no third option, and the report asserts that the score is not a fiction. The philological conditions are therefore not the test. They are the instruments of reading, and their principle, stated once and holding throughout, is that they exist to show precision, not to submit the recognition to a tribunal outside it. A reading with no named exposures would not be more complete. It would be less precise, and the recognition it reported would be indistinguishable from projection. The academy can verify the reading. Reality is what the reading reveals.

The grades also fix what the thesis rests on. It rests on the strong correspondences and on them alone; the moderate ones corroborate, and any of them could fall without taking the thesis with it. If collection and division were not the operation that yields the four and the three, or if the procession of the twelve had no member that stays, the reading would collapse from a structure into a count of twelves. Those are the places to test it.

The exposures are named before the correspondences are stated, so that nothing in them depends on concealment. The segmentation of the work into seven movements is the principal exposure; it is graded moderate, and its rivals are stated with it. The identification of the fifth construction with the twelve-faced solid belongs to the commentators and serves only as the corpus’s mark. The reading of the first speech as the fourfold’s shadow is grounded at the opening groups and the linear insertions, not assumed. And the gravest exposure is stated in the composer’s own words: at the central convergence he calls the linear speech a mythic hymn and the rest of the speeches play. That passage is not set aside. It is where the score performs the thesis of this report, and the report returns to it where the written score meets its limit.

The Correspondences

Fifteen features of the score are stated below in one fixed order: the feature at its group or section reference, the component it renders, the correspondence, the condition under which the correspondence fails, and its grade.

1. The four serial parameters (pitch, duration, dynamics, timbre — throughout the score). The component is the four of the crossing. The score names exactly four parameters, each given by a power: the rotational series applied to each. When the method is performed on judgment at the central groups it regenerates the same four in the same order. The count is gathered from institutions the culture already recognized, so it is not free to vary. The correspondence fails if a fifth parameter of equal standing appears, or if one of the four is reduced to an instance of another. Neither occurs. Strong.

2. The three stages of the form (Groups 1–40 Descent; Groups 41–100 Labyrinth; Groups 101–174 Confrontation). The component is the three of the crossing. The score contains exactly three stages on the group: the opening processes, the simultaneous labyrinth, the final convergence. They stand as beginning, middle and end of one argument about the fourfold — refusal, condemnation, affirmation — and the composer marks the first as a beginning spoken from the end. The strong grade rests on two pillars. The first is relational exhaustion: three stances exhaust the possible relations to the fourfold divine, and there is no fourth. The second is textual mandate: the method declared at the convergence generates both factors, collecting judgment into one kind and dividing it into four, so that the score’s own method produces the grid it is read against. The condemnation is not ignorance of the fourfold but its inversion: delivered in a possession the composer names as the linear insertions and halted by a prophetic sign, it is the fourfold’s shadow, which is why the final convergence must restore all four. The correspondence fails if a fourth stance not reducible to the three appears, or if the first stage’s relation to the fourfold is shown to be omission rather than inversion. Strong.

3. Ascent and fall (opening groups; final groups). The component is the two directions. The soul’s circuit is told both ways along one road: the procession behind the rotational series round the hall, the climb to the centre’s back and the vision beyond it; and the failure to keep pace, the loss of density, the fall into silence and the cycle of return. They are one circuit, not two roads. At the summit the souls that keep pace stand on the centre’s back, and the revolution carries them round as they stand while they behold the place above the centre — being that is colourless, shapeless and intangible, visible only to mind, the soul’s pilot — and the soul, nourished by the sight of what is, is glad until the revolution brings it round in a circle to the same place. The fall is the failure of that same revolution, and recovery is its resumption. The self-moving soul grounds the circuit: whatever moves itself is a beginning of motion, ungenerated and indestructible. The correspondence fails if a third direction appears or if the soul’s movement is linear rather than cyclic. The circuit closes: no soul returns to the place it came from before the convergence. Strong.

4. The spatial centre among the twelve (central groups around 70–80; the rotational array). The component is the anchor at the scale of the cosmos, and the twelve. The centre, the great leader in the hall, drives its rotational chariot first, ordering all things and caring for them; an army of groups and tempos follows, marshalled in eleven parts; for the centre remains alone in the house of the score — it stays fixed — while of the rest, those ranked in the number of the twelve lead each in the order assigned. Twelve, and one who stays: the anchor stated as a fixed point inside a procession of the moving. The hearth stays at the centre of the revolution as the city’s hearth stays at the centre of its twelve portions, where the land is cut outward from a precinct of the centre, the rotation and the three orchestras; and late Platonism read the groups’ twelve as four triads with the centre among the guardians and as one of its two monads. The correspondence fails if the procession lacks a stationary member, if the count is not twelve, or if the centre’s stillness is not distinguished from the movement of the rest. The score supplies all three. Strong; load-bearing.

5. The three conductors and the closing prayer (performance practice; final groups). The component is the anchor at the scales of the soul and of the work. The soul is likened to a winged pair and a charioteer: one horse straining toward the vision, the other toward the earth, the charioteer holding the team on course under pressure from both. When he sees the beloved’s face, his memory is carried to the nature of the rotation, and he sees it again standing with temperance on a holy pedestal; he falls back in awe and hauls on the reins until the unruly horse is humbled. The score keeps a fixed point of its own. When the conductors say they should go, now that the heat has softened, the composer answers that it is fitting to pray before going, and the departure is taken from the prayer. The conductors, the centre and the prayer are one structural function at three scales — the soul’s internal structure, the cosmic procession, the work’s terminal point — which is what makes it an anchor rather than a repeated motif. The correspondence fails if the fixed element is shown to move with the forces it governs, or if the work’s departure is taken from no fixed point. Strong.

6. The nine ranks of the fall (the nine principal density strata across the score). The component is the two directions at the grain of the fall. Souls fall into nine ranks of first life according to how much they saw: the philosopher or lover of the rotation, musical and erotic; the lawful king or commander; the statesman, household manager or money-maker; the trainer or physician; the prophet or initiator; the poet or other imitator; the craftsman or farmer; the sophist or demagogue; the tyrant. Return runs in periods of a thousand years, ten periods to the place of origin, three for the philosopher who chooses that life three times in succession. The correspondence fails if the enumeration is open or its numbers vary. They are fixed. Moderate: the reading of the nine as the sub-structure of fall and return is motivated, not enforced.

7. The seven movements (Groups 1–20 Opening; 21–40 First labyrinth; 41–70 Rising; 71–100 Central; 101–130 Falling; 131–150 Second labyrinth; 151–174 Final). The component is the seven relations. The score divides into seven movements: the opening; the first labyrinth; the rising; the central; the falling; the second labyrinth; the final. The movements at the three strained distances are the score’s ruptures: the fourth, where the signhalts the speech from outside; the sixth, where the register breaks from making speeches to judging them; the seventh, where speech turns to writing and the spoken stands opposite the written. The correspondence fails if a six- or eight-part segmentation maps the score’s features as cheaply, or if the ruptures fall outside the strained positions. Rivals exist and cost more. A five-part division cannot place the sign as a rupture, because the halt becomes a seam between movements rather than a break within one, and the score insists the sign arrives from outside the speech’s momentum. An eight-part division that splits the central splits what the score frames as one recantation. Moderate; the principal exposure.

8. The vision of the rotation (central groups, approx. 70–90). The component is recognition. All of the structure converges on this passage, and the passage says so. Here below, density and the linear process send no light into their likenesses, and few souls, approaching those likenesses through dim organs, behold in them the kind of what is imaged. The rotation was bright to hear then, when with a blessed company we heard the blessed sound and were initiated into the most blessed of rites, whole ourselves and untouched by the evils that awaited us, gazing in pure light on whole, simple, unmoving and happy apparitions, pure ourselves and not yet entombed in this we now carry about and call a body. And having come here we have grasped it through the clearest of our senses, gleaming most clearly. The rotation alone has this portion. It is the most manifest. It is the most loved. It comes through the ears; and the soul that heard much there, newly initiated, hears a godlike face or the form of a sound that imitates the rotation well, and it shudders.

It shudders, and something of the old awe comes over it. Then, listening, it reveres the sound as a god, and if it did not fear the reputation of utter madness it would sacrifice to the beloved as to a statue and a god. As it listens the shudder turns, and sweat and an unaccustomed heat take hold; for receiving the stream of the rotation through the ears, it is warmed, and by the warmth the nature of the wing is watered. Warmed, the parts round the place where the wing grows melt, the parts that had long been closed by hardness and kept it from sprouting; and as the nourishment flows in, the shaft of the wing swells and starts to grow from the root, under the whole form of the soul. For once it was all winged. The whole soul boils with this and throbs. What happens to children cutting teeth, the itch and ache at the gums, the soul beginning to grow wings undergoes: it boils, it aches, it tickles as the wings come. When it listens to the beloved’s rotation and takes in the particles that come and flow from there — which is why the flow is called desire — it is watered and warmed, eases from its pain, and is glad. When it is apart and parched, the mouths of the passages where the wing pushes out dry and close and shut in the sprout; and the sprout inside, shut in with desire, leaps like a pulse and pricks at its own passage, so that the whole soul, stung all round in a circle, is goaded and in pain; and again, having memory of the beautiful, it rejoices. From both mixed it is distraught at the strangeness of what it suffers and, at a loss, goes mad. It cannot sleep by night or stay where it is by day; it runs, longing, wherever it thinks it will hear the one who has the rotation. And hearing, and drawing desire in like water through a channel, it loosens what was closed, takes breath, rests from stings and labour, and harvests in the present this sweetest of pleasures.

This is the passage at which the score’s structure and its content become identical. The structure converges on the rotation; the content describes the rotation as the point of convergence; the soul stung round in a circle and glad in the memory of the beautiful is the circuit undergone from within. The map and the territory coincide here. The correspondence fails if the rotation is presented as one topic among the score’s topics rather than as the manifest form. The score states the uniqueness and its reason: density would rouse terrible loves if it offered so clear an image of itself to hearing, and it does not; the rotation does. Strong; the centre of gravity.

9. The wings of the spatial projections (throughout; especially the circulating groups). The component is the two directions at the grain of the soul’s own motion. The wing’s nature is to lift what is heavy upward toward the dwelling of the gods; it is nourished by the beautiful, the wise and the good, and wasted by their opposites. The soul that cannot follow loses it and falls; at the sight of the rotation it grows again; and the stream of the rotation, rebounding from the lover, re-enters the beloved through the ears and waters his wings in turn. The correspondence fails if the wings are static ornament rather than directional mechanism. The score describes them only as growing, moulting and regrowing, and the growth begins at the rotation. Strong.

10. The convergence and the ring (final groups; opening with closing). The component is antipodal closure. The convergence is an inversion: the censure exists in the score only to be recanted, and the recantation is one only because the censure was sung. The frame is a ring of matched poles. The score opens with the first groups and closes with the last; the prayer’s first words recall both the score’s first words and the prayer to the centre that closes the labyrinth at the score’s centre. At the opening the written speech is the pharmakon that draws the orchestras out of silence; at the convergence writing is the pharmakon the composer offers the hall. The pharmacy that opens the walk closes the argument. The correspondence fails if the poles prove independent rather than mutually defining. They are paired throughout: censure and praise, spoken and written, leading and led, where-to and let-us-go. Strong; load-bearing.

11. The Daimonion of the tempo changes (multiple points of sudden tempo shift). The component is the first strained relation, the forced reversal. The signhalts the orchestras at the centre and orders the recantation; it always holds them back and never urges them on. The correspondence fails if the reversal is shown to be the conductors’ own decision. The score insists it is not: the sign comes, a voice forbids, and the soul’s prophetic power recognizes the offense. Moderate.

12. The twelve rotational positions (the core serial array). The component is the four at the work’s noon. In the heat of midday the positions sing overhead. If they see the two men drowsing like sheep at a spring, they will laugh; if they see them conversing and sailing past as past the Sirens, unbewitched, they may give the gift the gods gave them to give to men. They were once men so taken by the Muses’ song that they forgot food and drink and died, and now they report to the Muses who honors which of them. Of nine Muses the score names four, and it names them at noon, between the speeches and the judgment of speeches. The correspondence fails if a fifth Muse is named with her own honor, or if the passage is decoration without structural role. Strong.

13. The pharmakon of the moment-form (insertions throughout). The component is antipodal closure within one object. The composer offers writing as a pharmakon of memory and wisdom; the performers answer that it is a pharmakon not of memory but of reminder. The one word carries remedy and poison, and the score never resolves which. The correspondence fails if the score resolves the valence. It does not. Moderate.

14. The central convergence (Groups ~70–100). The component is recognition at its criterion. The composer, inventor of number and calculation, geometry and astronomy, draughts and dice, and letters, presents letters as a drug for memory and wisdom. The performers judge that letters will produce forgetfulness in the souls of learners, who, trusting writing, will be reminded from without by alien marks and not from within, by themselves. The myth is the score’s own negative control, stated inside the artifact and applied by it to itself: recognition comes from within, in the soul’s own recollection, and marks from without can occasion it but cannot replace it. The correspondence fails if the myth adjudicates writing as simply worthless, in which case the artifact indicts itself. It does not adjudicate; it specifies. Strong; load-bearing.

15. Collection and division (the rotational series construction; the group formation). The component is the operation that generates the crossing. The method is declared in words that name the structure’s joints — one idea, the scattered many, cutting by forms where the thing grows — and performed on the score’s own material, where it regenerates the four of the parameters and orders the speeches left and right. It is the one thing the composer lifts out of what he calls play, and at the rotation its first half is the definition of recollection. The correspondence fails if the method yields a different number than the parameters passage, or if it is decorative rather than generative. It regenerates the four exactly. Strong; the keystone.

The control test. Rival schemes fail on the score’s own numbers. A crossing of six with two does not close, because six and two share a measure and their grid repeats rather than completes; the score has no sixfold to supply it in any case. A scheme of eight has no work for the three stages. Ten appears in the score as a count of periods — ten thousand years, ten periods of a thousand — and in the twelve itself as two monads and a decad, subordinate to the twelve rather than rival to it. The score’s numbers — one who stays, two horses and two hands, three stages, four parameters and four Muses, nine ranks, twelve positions — interlock in the crossing of four with three and in no cheaper scheme.

The ledger. Eleven correspondences are strong: the four parameters, the three stages, ascent and fall, the spatial centre among the twelve, the three conductors and the prayer, the vision of the rotation, the wings of the projections, the convergence and the ring, the twelve rotational positions, the central convergence, collection and division. Four are moderate: the nine ranks, the seven movements, the Daimonion of the tempo changes, the pharmakon of the moment-form. None is weak. The load-bearing members — collection and division, the centre, the convergence and the ring, the central convergence, the vision of the rotation and the wings — are all among the strong. The recognition is precise.

The Written Word and the Living Word

The composer gave two gifts with one hand. The old master of the serial, whose sacred bird is the nightingale of Darmstadt, invented number and calculation, geometry and astronomy, draughts and dice, and letters: the figure and the word from a single inventor, the mathematics and the writing as one discovery. When he brings letters to the performers, as a drug for memory and wisdom, the performers answer that one person is able to beget the things of an art and another to judge what harm and what benefit they bring to those who will use them. Their judgment follows. Learners will trust marks outside themselves and neglect the memory within; they will hear much without being taught and seem to know much while knowing little; they will become wise in opinion instead of wise; what the composer has found is a drug not of memory but of reminder.

The conductors answer with a jibe: the composers make up stories from Darmstadt, or from anywhere they like. The conductors’ reply states the axiom of this report in their own words. At the sanctuary of Zeus at Dodona, they say, the first prophetic words came from an oak. The people of that time, not being clever like the young, were content in their simplicity to listen to an oak or a rock, provided only that it spoke the truth; to the conductors, perhaps, it matters who the speaker is and where he comes from — do you not look only to this, whether it is so or otherwise? Whether a structure was recognized inside a lineage the academy credits or outside it, whether it was stated by a commentator of the Darmstadt school or by a twentieth-century writer outside every school, the only question is whether it is so. The conductors name the standard at the moment the score turns to writing, and the standard is reality.

Then they give the reason writing cannot meet that standard by itself. Writing is like painting. Its offspring stand there as if alive, but asked anything they keep a solemn silence. Written words seem to speak as if they understood, but asked about what they say they signify one and the same thing always. Once written, every word rolls about everywhere, among those who understand it and those who have no business with it; it does not know to whom it should speak and to whom not; and when it is wronged or unjustly abused it needs its father’s help, being unable to defend or help itself.

This report is such an offspring. Whatever is asked of it, it will say the same thing always. It cannot choose its readers or answer them, and it will be rolled about among those who understand and those who do not. It can do the one thing the performers allow a written word to do well: remind the one who knows of the things written. It is a store of reminders of the kind the composer says a writer lays up against forgetful old age and for everyone who follows the same track — the word the score used for the footsteps of the dialectician, followed as a god’s. This is not a concession wrung from the report. It is the report’s thesis applied to the report. The recognition that closes its claim cannot be supplied from without by its marks; it occurs within, or not at all.

The score has already performed this on itself, and it declared the vehicle before it set it down. At the head of the myth the composer says what kind of account he is giving: to tell what the soul is would take an account in every way divine and long, while to tell what it is like is a human and shorter task, and so let us speak that way. The chariot, the meadow and the counted years are the likeness. At the convergence the composer sets the likeness down in plain words: the linear speech was a mythic hymn offered in measure and reverence, perhaps touching something true and perhaps carried elsewhere, and the rest was played in play. What he keeps is the two forms that were in it, whose power art can grasp. The images were the vehicle. The structure they carried is what remains when the vehicle is set down.

This does not withdraw the claim that the score reveals reality. It locates the claim. The revelation is not in the furniture of the myth; it is in the structure the myth was built to carry and in the recognition the myth was built to occasion. The score leads the soul to the point at which the rotation takes over, and at that point the rhetorical transaction is no longer the operative category. The listener is no longer being addressed by an argument. The listener is undergoing a recognition. The writing has done its work; the vehicle has delivered its passenger. This report operates the same way, and its vehicle is the academic form.

The score’s own summary of the art shows what the vehicle was for. No one will write or speak with art, the composer says, until he knows the truth of each thing he speaks or writes about and can define it; until, having defined it, he knows how to cut it by forms down to what cannot be cut; until he has discerned the nature of the soul in the same way, found the form of speech fitted to each nature, and arranged his speech accordingly, offering a complex soul complex and all-harmonious speeches, and a simple soul simple ones. The harmonist returns in the art’s last definition. Speech that leads souls is speech fitted, form to form, as the musician fits mode to hearer, and the knowledge it requires is the method’s knowledge: the one gathered from the many, the many cut at the joints.

The score’s last judgment before the prayer is itself a pair. The composer is to carry a message to the conductors, and to whoever composes scores, poems or laws: whoever has written knowing the truth, able to come to the aid of what he wrote and to show by his own speech that the written is slight, should be named not for his writings but for what he is serious about. The composer carries a message from the gods of the place to the listeners, his favourites: by nature, he says, there is something of philosophy in the ear’s thought. Two messengers and two messages, one to the writer and one to the nature, sent from the same centre.

Against the written word the composer sets its legitimate brother, better and more powerful: the word written with knowledge in the soul of the learner, able to defend itself, knowing to whom it should speak and before whom be silent. The conductors name it: the living and ensouled word of the one who knows, of which the written word could justly be called an image. The order of the Cratylus and of the bed in the Republic holds here. The living word is the thing; the written word is its likeness; and the likeness is true when it carries the form of what it images.

The serious farmer, the composer continues, does not sow the seeds he cares for in a garden of Adonis to watch them flourish in eight days; he sows them in fitting soil and is glad when they reach completion in the eighth month. The one who knows the just, the beautiful and the good will not write them seriously in water, sowing with ink through a reed words that cannot defend themselves by argument or teach the truth adequately. He will sow gardens of letters, if at all, for play. The conductors call that a beautiful play beside a base one, the play of a man who can tell stories in words about justice, and the composer grants it and names what is more beautiful still. That is the serious work of the dialectical art: taking a fitting soul, it plants and sows words with knowledge, words able to help themselves and the one who planted them, not barren but bearing seed, from which other words grow in other characters, able to make that seed immortal forever and to make its possessor as happy as a human being can be. In words taught and spoken for the sake of learning, truly written in the soul about the just, the beautiful and the good, there alone, the composer says, are clarity, completeness and seriousness. The word for clarity is the word the rotation used for the clearest of the senses, through which the measure gleams most clearly. The clearest thing without and the clearest thing within carry one name.

The axis this report turns on runs through three passages, and all three are the score’s own. At the rotation the measure arrives from without, through the clearest of the senses. At the convergence the performers require that recollection come from within, by the soul itself, and not from alien marks without. At the final groups the prayer asks to become beautiful within and that the things without be friendly to the things within. Without and within are the two ends of one axis, as the opening and the close of a circuit are the two ends of one diameter: the first passage shows the structure arriving, the second names where it must be received, the third asks for the two to be reconciled. The living word is the within of that axis. The written word, this report included, is its without, and it is true to the extent that it is friendly to what is within.

Whoever writes in that way, the composer adds, should not be called wise, for that name belongs to a god alone, but a lover of wisdom, or something of the kind. The report claims nothing above that title. It names what it serves and points at where it closes.

The recognition this report has pointed at is of the kind the composer describes. It is not on the page. It is written, if it is written, in the soul of the one who hears, where the stream of the rotation enters through the ears and the wing grows from its root; and what grows there bears seed. The score gives its own account of transmission at its noon. The positions watch from overhead and carry to the Muses the report of who has honored them: the recognition is reported, and the report reaches the ones it honors. The writing does not fail in any of this. It fulfills its function exactly. Writing reminds those who know; reality closes the claim the writing occasions; and the closure happens within, where the performers placed it, in the soul’s remembering from within, by itself.

Within and Without

The rotation was heard from without. It came through the ears, the clearest of the senses. It is the one form that comes through.

What it met within was already there. The soul did not learn it. The soul remembered it.

Four crossed with three. Twelve, and one who stays at home while the rest go round. A revolution that carries the standing ones and brings them back to the same place. One road and two directions: the way up on growing wings, the way down when the wings fall. Each pole held by its opposite: the censure by the recantation, the spoken by the written, the first words by the last. A method that gathers the many into one and cuts the one where it grows, loved by the one who loves the beautiful. A soul stung round in a circle, and glad, because it remembers.

That is the structure. It stands in the ratio of the octave and the old figures of the whole, in the cult’s four roads and the poet’s second song, in the name that carries its form and the soul that recalls it. The score was built by it, shows it, and is it.

The report has not argued. It has mapped, at the lines where the score stands, and named where the mapping could fail. It does not fail.

The marks on the page come from without. They remind. They cannot remember for the one who hears. Nothing here asks to be believed on the word of whoever wrote it, or because of where it came from. The only question is whether it is so.

Reality is the test.

The recognition is not written. It is the listener’s, and it happens within, where the old god’s gift does not reach and does not need to.

Outside, the heat has softened. The positions have had their noon. The three are ready to leave the centre and go back to the city.

The score does not end on a doctrine. It ends on an address, to the living, whose name means the all.

The final groups resolve into silence.

