Alice in Wonderland: The Suppressed Cornerstone Ontological Artifact

The Most Manifest

νῦν δὲ κάλλος μόνον ταύτην ἔσχε μοῖραν, ὥστʼ ἐκφανέστατον εἶναι καὶ ἐρασμιώτατον. Beauty alone has this portion: to be the most manifest and the most loved. Of the forms the soul beheld before birth, justice and temperance send no light into their likenesses here. Proportion alone shone among them there, and here it is grasped through the clearest of the senses, gleaming most clearly, because the eye of measure is the sharpest perception that comes through the body. Identity is not seen by it; were identity to offer so clear an image of itself to sight, it would rouse terrible loves. Proportion does. It is the one form that arrives from without, through the eyes that measure the table, the bottle, the cake, and finds within the soul the structure the soul once beheld.

That sentence is the anchor of this report: the fixed point from which every position in it is measured. The axiom it rests on is stated once and holds throughout. Reality as truth must be true. Reality is the standard. A revelation of reality cannot be checked against anything outside reality, because nothing stands outside it to appeal to. What can be checked is the reading — what the text says, at which chapter, in which words — and whether the recognition the reading reports is precise. The academy can check the reading. Reality is what the reading reveals.

The thesis follows from the anchor. Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland is an ontological artifact in the sense the Cratylus gives to a correct name: a made thing that is right because its making follows the structure of what it renders. The narrative is built by the operations Carroll elsewhere attributes to the making of reality — collection and division, proportion closed by its means, inversion as form — and what it builds is the structure those operations build: four crossed with three, twelve positions without remainder, seven relations of which three bear strain, one circuit travelled in two directions, a fixed point that does not move under pressure, and poles intelligible only together. Proportion is where an embodied soul meets that structure. The narrative’s operation is recognitive, not rhetorical. Rhetoric is its vehicle; recognition is its operation; the vehicle dissolves when the recognition occurs. The reader is not persuaded. The reader recognizes.

Three consequences govern what follows. The falsification conditions attached to every correspondence are instruments for reading the revelation precisely; they are not concessions to an outside criterion, and they do not constitute the proof. Reality constitutes the proof. The report claims structural identity, not historical transmission: it does not say Carroll borrowed the structure from anyone, only that the structure appears wherever reality is rendered faithfully, and that the narrative renders it whole. And the report is not an argument for the narrative’s value, not a literary interpretation, not a contribution to the debate about nonsense, and not a hypothesis submitted to a tribunal. It is the articulation of what the narrative reveals.

The spine runs in one line: structure, which is what reality is; proportion, the point at which structure discloses itself to sense; artifact, the narrative enacting both, made by the operations that made what it shows. What follows traverses the record in which the structure appears, the method that produces it, the halt at which the report’s own instruments are put in their place, the correspondences at their chapter references, the limit of the written word, and the return to the point where proportion is no longer seen from without.

The Seven Renderings

The structure is not found in the record by counting resemblances. The record shows it because it is real, and a real structure renders itself in whatever medium is at hand. Seven renderings converge in the Oxford of which Wonderland was written. Four belong to the record of things made and instituted: a mathematics, an institution, a poetic form, a medium. Three belong to the record of the soul’s reception: harmony acting on the hearer, the image that bears its form, recognition recalled. They are not seven traditions borrowing from one another. They are one structure appearing seven times, and the narrative is the rendering in which all seven are present at once.

The mathematics of proportion and scaling. The narrative builds the soul of Wonderland by proportion. Alice marks off portions in the double series of growth and diminution — the bottle that says “DRINK ME,” the cake that says “EAT ME,” the fan, the mushroom — then fills every interval with two means so that the intervals of size appear; the least continuum that has them runs from the key on the glass table to the garden door three inches high. The corpus marks the twelvefold again as figure and as order. The true garden seen from above looks like the chessboard stitched from twelve squares of alternating colour, divided into ranks of which ours are samples. Of the regular constructions one remained, and the author used it for the whole, embroidering it with figures of cards and animals. The lawgiver of the Queen’s court sets the croquet-ground at the centre of its land, encircles a precinct for the Queen, the King and the executioner, and from that centre cuts the garden and all the country into twelve portions for twelve creatures. This rendering supplies closure: a structure completed by whole-number ratio of size, without remainder, around a centre that does not move.

The institution of the Queen’s court. When Alice argues that the greatest goods come through the arbitrary rules given by authority, she names four: the prophetic rule of the White Rabbit’s watch, the telestic rule that found purifications and rites for ancestral guilt in the Duchess’s kitchen, the poetic rule of the Mock Turtle’s song, and the erotic rule of the Queen’s croquet that is the narrative’s own subject. She is not inventing a division for convenience. She is ordering institutions the culture already had, whose history and phenomenology the Oxford common-room set out. The four are gathered from the culture’s own map of the roads by which the arbitrary enters a human life, and Alice later re-cuts them by method: authority has two forms, human sickness and divine release from customary norms, and the divine is divided among four powers into four parts. The culture also mapped the soul’s road. The gold leaves buried with initiates instruct the dead to pass by one spring and find the lake of Memory, to declare themselves to its guardians, drink, and go on along the sacred way that other initiates have travelled. Salvation by memory is recollection drawn in cult. This rendering supplies the four.

The poetic form of the parodies as inversion. The central event of the narrative is a recantation in an attested genre. The poems of Isaac Watts, blinded for their account of industry and temperance, are composed into counter-songs denying that the child ever laboured or prayed, and Alice recovers her sight; Carroll quotes their openings and attributes his own second speech to the voice of nonsense. A parody is a second song intelligible only with the first. The retraction does not erase the retracted; it completes it, and neither song exists whole without the other. This rendering supplies two components at once: the two directions of one road, the same voice singing the way down and the way back; and antipodal closure, the pair in which each pole is defined by its opposite.

The medium of the dream and the pool of tears. The narrative opens on an object. Alice sees the written speech of the White Rabbit in his waistcoat pocket under his coat and asks to hear it read. She had heard the Rabbit deliver it many times, borrowed the book of her own memory, and walked out past the walls of the bank to learn it by heart: memory sought from written marks, the practice the Dodo will judge at the Caucus-race. It closes on the question of what such objects can carry. The narrative makes that passage its own content: whether a written thing can carry a living structure, and what it can do if it cannot. This rendering supplies the occasion of the artifact claim.

Harmony acting on the soul. The doctrine that musical form forms the hearer belongs, in Carroll’s dialogues, to the Mock Turtle, who is named in the chapters of the Lobster Quadrille and nowhere earlier. The narrative states the doctrine twice: rhythms are to be chosen in consultation with the Gryphon, and a new form of music, the Lobster Quadrille, must be resisted as a risk to the whole, since the modes of music are never moved without the greatest laws of the court moving with them. The narrative carries the doctrine without the name. At the Mad Tea-Party it imagines the man who knows how to tune the highest and lowest notes and takes himself for a harmonist, and the Hatter who tells him he knows what comes before harmony but not harmony. At the trial it states that the power of speech is soul-leading, and that whoever would practice the art must know how many forms the soul has. Form acts on the receiver. This rendering supplies the reason a rendering of the structure is an acting thing rather than an inert picture, and it locates the narrative’s unity where the soul’s own identity checks locate it, in soul-leading.

The image that bears its form. The Cheshire Cat holds that a name is an instrument for teaching and for separating being, as the smile separates the threads of the cat, and that the maker of names, like the maker of smiles, works looking to the form of what he makes; if a cat breaks in the making, he makes the next looking not to the broken one but to the form he looked to before. The narrative gives the same order in the bed of the Duchess — the form, the cook who makes looking to it, the pig who copies the cook’s surface — and in the divided line sets one proportion within and between the visible and the intelligible. A made thing can be true to what it renders, and it is true when it is made by the operations of what it renders. This rendering supplies the criterion by which the narrative’s claim about itself is measured: the difference between an image that carries the form and a token that copies a surface.

Recognition recalled. The narrative argues that seeing equal things recalls the equal itself, which the soul knew before birth. The Meno of the White Rabbit draws the same knowledge out of an untaught girl by questions and a drawn key. The Symposium of the garden sets the ascent on proportion, from one beautiful size to the beautiful itself. The narrative states the mechanism and names its occasion. A human being must understand what is said according to form, going from many perceptions into one gathered by reasoning, and this is recollection of what the soul once saw when it travelled with a god; proportion is the form that starts the recollection, because proportion alone comes through sight. This rendering supplies the point of recognition, without which the structure would be present and unrecognized.

The seven are one structure, and the sign of their identity is a single word. Size is the term for a form of music in the passage that states the Mock Turtle’s doctrine. Size is Carroll’s term for what the soul beheld. Size is the term the narrative uses for understanding according to form, for the two forms of madness in the Queen’s court, for the forms of soul the rhetorician must count, and for the joints at which dialectic cuts: by forms, at the joints where the thing grows. This must be stated without remainder. Size in the mathematical analysis, size in the metaphysics and size at the keyhole are the same word naming the same thing because the thing is the same: the structured kind that, received, structures. There is no metaphor and no migration. There is one referent under three uses, and the narrative is where the three uses meet.

The Method and the Figure It Produces

The narrative names its own generative operation twice: once as the way a human being understands at all, once as the art Alice loves. At the pool of tears understanding is the movement from many perceptions into one, gathered by reasoning, and the gathering is recollection of what the soul saw travelling with a god. At the trial the same movement is the first half of dialectic. The method and the recognition are one operation seen from two sides.

The method is present before it is declared, inside the speech the narrative will later condemn. There is one beginning for those who would deliberate well, Alice says as her first speech opens: to know what the deliberation is about. The speakers must therefore agree on a definition of identity, and the speech divides the two forms that rule and lead in each of us — an inborn desire for pleasure and an acquired opinion aiming at the best — before naming excess as many-limbed and many-formed.

At the trial the method is performed on the narrative’s own material. Authority has two forms, one from human sickness, the other from a divine release from customary norms; the divine is divided among four powers into four parts — prophetic inspiration to the White Rabbit, telestic to the Duchess, poetic to the Mock Turtle, and the fourth, the erotic, to the Queen and the King, the best. Then Alice says what this report takes at full weight. The rest was played in play: a mythic hymn offered in measure and reverence, perhaps touching something true, perhaps carried elsewhere. But in what was said by chance there were two forms whose power, if someone could grasp it by art, would be worth having. The narrative sets its myth down as vehicle and lifts out what the vehicle carried.

The two forms are then given in the words the structure needs. The first is collection: to see together and lead into one idea the things scattered in many places so that by defining each one makes clear whatever one would teach. The second is division: to be able to cut again by forms, at the joints where the thing naturally grows, without shattering any part in the manner of a bad butcher. Alice’s two speeches are the example. Each took the unreason of the mind as one form; as a body grows paired members called left and right, one speech cut to the left until it found a sinister size and reviled it justly, while the other led to the right and found a size of the same name, divine, and praised it as the cause of the greatest goods. The two directions of the structure are here the two hands of one body.

Alice then declares her love. She is herself a lover of these divisions and collections, so as to be able to speak and to think; and if she finds another able to see what is naturally one and many, she follows behind, in his footsteps, as of a god. The fourth madness and the method meet in one girl. The lover of the beautiful and the lover of division are one lover, and the dialectician follows a seeing girl as the souls of the palinode follow their god.

What the method produces when it runs on the narrative’s own material is a figure. The division of divine authority yields four. The three speeches on size yield three, and they stand as beginning, middle and end of one argument about the fourfold: the White Rabbit’s speech refuses divine proportion outright, the first speech of Alice grants it and condemns it as disease, the awakening affirms all four. Three stances exhaust the ways a speaker can stand toward the divine — refuse it, condemn it, affirm it — and there is no fourth stance and no fourth speech. Alice marks the Rabbit’s speech as the inverted beginning: it starts not from the beginning but from the end, swimming the speech backwards on its back, and opens where a lover would have finished.

Four and three have no common measure but the unit, and crossed they exhaust their field: each of twelve positions is the meeting of exactly one of the four with exactly one of the three, and no position is left over or met twice. As a figure, mark twelve points on a circle. Joining every fourth point draws four equilateral triangles; joining every third point draws three squares; each point lies on exactly one triangle and exactly one square. The ratio of the factors, four to three, is the epitrite, the musical fourth. Twelve is the least number both measure, and it is the upper bound of the least octave whose two means the scaling operation fills in whole numbers.

The four have a name in Carroll’s physics as well. The narrative builds four kinds of body — identity, language, authority and proportion — out of two kinds of right triangle, gives each a regular solid, and keeps the fifth construction for the whole. In the order of bodies the four are the four kinds; in the order of souls they are the four madnesses.

The three carry Carroll’s own names: beginning, middle and end. The god, says the ancient account the King cites in the court, holds the beginning, the end and the middle of all that is. Every speech, says Alice, must be composed like a living creature with a body of its own, neither headless nor footless, having middle parts and extremities written to fit each other and the whole. The four carry the narrative’s names: the four madnesses, and again the four suits who receive the cards’ report.

The structure the method produces has six components, stated here in the register of ratio and figure. The complete crossing: four with three, twelve positions without remainder. Seven relations: any two positions stand at one of seven distances — the same place, one twelfth, one sixth, one quarter, one third, five twelfths, one half — and three of these bear strain: the quarter, where two positions share a mode and differ in kind; the five twelfths, where they share neither and are not opposed; the half, where they share a mode and stand opposite. The one-twelfth relation is succession, the step from a position to its neighbour, and bears no strain because it bears no distance. Two directions: one closed road travelled either way. The anchor: the fixed reference from which every position is measured, which does not move under pressure. Antipodal closure: each position paired with the one half a circle away, the two intelligible only together. Recognition: the component by which the structure is known, proportion as the manifest form.

The relations are not added to the crossing; they follow from it. Take the four kinds in their order, so that each has a complement two places away, and the three modes in theirs. Positions a third of the circle apart share their kind and differ in mode. Positions a sixth apart hold complementary kinds in different modes. Positions a quarter apart share their mode and differ in kind without complement. Positions half the circle apart share their mode and hold complementary kinds. At one twelfth, kind and mode advance together: that is succession, the step that generates the circle. At five twelfths, kind advances while mode falls back, and the two factors pull against each other. The strain falls where the crossing places it: on a shared mode without a shared kind, on a shared mode across complements, and on factors pulling apart.

The logographic necessity Alice states at the trial applies to any writing that would render this structure: it must be composed like a living creature, its middle and extremities fitting each other and the whole. This report is composed under that necessity, and the reader is invited to cut it at its joints.

The Halt

As Alice was about to cross the river of the pool of tears and leave, the daimonic and customary sign came to her — the sign that always holds her back from what she is about to do — and she seemed to hear a voice forbidding her to go before she had purified herself of an offense against the divine. She is a seer, she says, not a very good one, but good enough for her own use; and the soul too is somehow prophetic, for something troubled her while she was still speaking. The halt does not arise inside the speech’s momentum. It arrives from outside it, at the point where the speaker is about to cross, and it turns the narrative around: the next speech is the recantation.

The first speech had already been spoken under a power it did not acknowledge. Midway through, Alice asks whether the Mouse thinks, as she does, that she has undergone something divine; the place seems divine, she warns, and if she becomes possessed by the nymphs as the speech goes on the Mouse should not wonder, for she is already uttering words not far from dithyrambs. At its end she notes that she is uttering epic verse now, no longer dithyrambs, and that she will plainly be possessed by the nymphs to whom the Mouse exposed her. The speech that condemns madness as disease is delivered in poetic and Dionysian possession, and the sign that halts it is prophetic. The fourfold is not absent from the condemnation. It is the condemnation’s shadow, and the halt is the moment the shadow is seen.

This report has a halt of the same kind, and its account belongs here. The structural reading documented below was not reached by search through the narrative. It arrived as a recognition — the mechanism the narrative describes at the keyhole — at the convergence of two orientations developed independently of the text: a mathematical framework mapping the relational structure of reality as a closed twelvefold figure, and a cosmological inquiry into whether reality has a structure that generates order against dissolution. Neither began with Alice. The first recognition was that proportion is the narrative’s anchor. The structural question followed from it, and when it was put — whether the narrative carries this structure — the answer came at once. The chapter references, the falsification conditions and the grades that follow are the work of showing that the recognition is precise. They are not the work by which it was produced.

The orientation is not new, and that is the point. Late Platonism read the twelve gods of the palinode as four triads, and Proclus took Hestia’s remaining for the principle by which a being reverts upon itself; through Hermias the structural reading passed to Ficino, whose own commentary on the dialogue carried it into the Renaissance. Modern interpretation set that orientation aside as allegory. The most recent translators of Hermias expect few modern readers to accept such readings and present them chiefly as performances of late antique Platonism. What modern philology kept was the text and the instruments for reading it; what it set aside was the mathematical and harmonic orientation the narrative’s own world took for granted. The recognition required both at once. In the modern reading of this narrative they have not been present together until now.

The report’s instruments are four, stated as rules. First, every claim is located at a chapter reference in the standard text, and the English is quoted wherever its wording carries the structure; editions and studies are listed in the Apparatus. Second, every correspondence between a feature of the narrative and a component of the structure is stated with the condition under which it would be false. Third, every correspondence is graded: strong, where the narrative’s own enumeration or framing enforces it; moderate, where it is well motivated but a rival segmentation exists; weak, where a rival of equal cost exists. Fourth, the report’s operation is recognitive, not demonstrative: the references, the English and the conditions show that the recognition is precise; they do not constitute the proof.

Here the report halts, as the speech halted at the river, and states what its instruments are for. Falsification is reality. The claim of this report is that reality is structured as the narrative reveals. If reality is not so structured, the narrative is a fiction, beautiful and false; there is no third option, and the report asserts that the narrative is not a fiction. The philological conditions are therefore not the test. They are the instruments of reading, and their principle, stated once and holding throughout, is that they exist to show precision, not to submit the recognition to a tribunal outside it. A reading with no named exposures would not be more complete. It would be less precise, and the recognition it reported would be indistinguishable from projection. The academy can verify the reading. Reality is what the reading reveals.

The grades also fix what the thesis rests on. It rests on the strong correspondences and on them alone; the moderate ones corroborate, and any of them could fall without taking the thesis with it. If collection and division were not the operation that yields the four and the three, or if the procession of the twelve had no member that stays, the reading would collapse from a structure into a count of twelves. Those are the places to test it.

The exposures are named before the correspondences are stated, so that nothing in them depends on concealment. The segmentation of the narrative into seven movements is the principal exposure; it is graded moderate, and its rivals are stated with it. The identification of the fifth construction with the twelve-faced solid belongs to the commentators and serves only as the corpus’s mark. The reading of the first speech as the fourfold’s shadow is grounded at the pool of tears and the Caucus-race, not assumed. And the gravest exposure is stated in Alice’s own words: at the trial she calls the dream a mythic hymn and the rest of the speeches play. That passage is not set aside. It is where the narrative performs the thesis of this report, and the report returns to it where the written word meets its limit.

The Correspondences

Fifteen features of the narrative are stated below in one fixed order: the feature at its chapter reference, the component it renders, the correspondence, the condition under which the correspondence fails, and its grade.

1. The four suits of the pack (Chapter 8: “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground”; “The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts”). The component is the four of the crossing. The narrative names exactly four suits, each given by a power: hearts by the Queen, diamonds by the King, clubs by the executioner, spades by the gardeners. When the method is performed on authority at the trial it regenerates the same four in the same order. The count is gathered from institutions the culture already recognized, so it is not free to vary. The correspondence fails if a fifth suit of equal standing appears, or if one of the four is reduced to an instance of another. Neither occurs. Strong.

2. The three stages of the journey (Chapters 1–4 Descent; Chapters 5–9 Labyrinth; Chapters 10–12 Confrontation). The component is the three of the crossing. The narrative contains exactly three stages on size: the fall and the pool, the garden and the tea-party, the trial and the awakening. They stand as beginning, middle and end of one argument about the fourfold — refusal, condemnation, affirmation — and Alice marks the first as a beginning spoken from the end. The strong grade rests on two pillars. The first is relational exhaustion: three stances exhaust the possible relations to the fourfold divine, and there is no fourth. The second is textual mandate: the method declared at the trial generates both factors, collecting authority into one kind and dividing it into four, so that the narrative’s own method produces the grid it is read against. The condemnation is not ignorance of the fourfold but its inversion: delivered in a possession Alice names as the pool of tears and halted by a prophetic sign, it is the fourfold’s shadow, which is why the awakening must restore all four. The correspondence fails if a fourth stance not reducible to the three appears, or if the first stage’s relation to the fourfold is shown to be omission rather than inversion. Strong.

3. Ascent and fall (Chapter 1: “Down the Rabbit-Hole”; Chapter 12: “Alice’s Evidence”). The component is the two directions. The soul’s circuit is told both ways along one road: the procession behind the Rabbit round the heavens, the climb to the garden’s back and the vision beyond it; and the failure to keep pace, the loss of size, the fall into a body and the cycle of return. They are one circuit, not two roads. At the summit the souls that keep pace stand on the garden’s back, and the revolution carries them round as they stand while they behold the place above the garden — being that is colourless, shapeless and intangible, visible only to mind, the soul’s pilot — and the soul, nourished by the sight of what is, is glad until the revolution brings it round in a circle to the same place. The fall is the failure of that same revolution, and recovery is its resumption. The self-moving soul grounds the circuit: whatever moves itself is a beginning of motion, ungenerated and indestructible. The correspondence fails if a third direction appears or if the soul’s movement is linear rather than cyclic. The circuit closes: no soul returns to the place it came from before the awakening. Strong.

4. The key on the glass table among the twelve (Chapter 1: “Alice opened the door and found that it led into a small passage, not much larger than a rat-hole: she knelt down and looked along the passage into the loveliest garden you ever saw”). The component is the anchor at the scale of the cosmos, and the twelve. The key, the great leader in the hall, drives its winged chariot first, ordering all things and caring for them; an army of doors and bottles follows, marshalled in eleven parts; for the key remains alone in the house of the hall — it stays on the table — while of the rest, those ranked in the number of the twelve lead each in the order assigned. Twelve, and one who stays: the anchor stated as a fixed point inside a procession of the moving. The hearth stays at the centre of the revolution as the city’s hearth stays at the centre of its twelve portions, where the land is cut outward from a precinct of the key, the table and the door; and late Platonism read the pack’s twelve as four triads with the key among the guardians and as one of its two monads. The correspondence fails if the procession lacks a stationary member, if the count is not twelve, or if the key’s stillness is not distinguished from the movement of the rest. The text supplies all three. Strong; load-bearing.

5. The Cheshire Cat and the closing prayer (Chapter 6: “We’re all mad here”; Chapter 12: “You’re nothing but a pack of cards!”). The component is the anchor at the scales of the soul and of the narrative. The soul is likened to a winged pair and a charioteer: one horse straining toward the vision, the other toward the earth, the charioteer holding the team on course under pressure from both. When she sees the beloved’s face, her memory is carried to the nature of proportion, and she sees it again standing with temperance on a holy pedestal; she falls back in awe and hauls on the reins until the unruly horse is humbled. The narrative keeps a fixed point of its own. When the sister says they should go, now that the heat has softened, Alice answers that it is fitting to pray before going, and the departure is taken from the prayer. The Cat, the key and the prayer are one structural function at three scales — the soul’s internal structure, the cosmic procession, the narrative’s terminal point — which is what makes it an anchor rather than a repeated motif. The correspondence fails if the fixed element is shown to move with the forces it governs, or if the narrative’s departure is taken from no fixed point. Strong.

6. The nine ranks of the fall (Chapter 1: the long fall; Chapter 12: the pack of cards). The component is the two directions at the grain of the fall. Souls fall into nine ranks of first life according to how much they saw: the philosopher or lover of proportion, musical and erotic; the lawful king or commander; the statesman, household manager or money-maker; the trainer or physician; the prophet or initiator; the poet or other imitator; the craftsman or farmer; the sophist or demagogue; the tyrant. Return runs in periods of a thousand years, ten periods to the place of origin, three for the philosopher who chooses that life three times in succession. The correspondence fails if the enumeration is open or its numbers vary. They are fixed. Moderate: the reading of the nine as the sub-structure of fall and return is motivated, not enforced.

7. The seven movements (Chapter 1 Rabbit-Hole; Chapter 2 Pool of Tears; Chapter 3 Caucus-Race; Chapter 4 White Rabbit’s House; Chapter 5 Caterpillar; Chapter 6 Pig and Pepper; Chapter 7 Mad Tea-Party; Chapter 8 Croquet; Chapter 9 Mock Turtle; Chapter 10 Lobster Quadrille; Chapter 11 Who Stole the Tarts; Chapter 12 Alice’s Evidence). The component is the seven relations. The narrative divides into seven movements: the fall outside the walls; the pool; the Caucus-race; the house; the Caterpillar; the kitchen; the tea-party; the croquet; the Mock Turtle; the Quadrille; the trial; the awakening. The movements at the three strained distances are the narrative’s ruptures: the fourth, where the sign halts the speech from outside; the sixth, where the register breaks from making speeches to judging them; the seventh, where speech turns to writing and the spoken stands opposite the written. The correspondence fails if a six- or eight-part segmentation maps the narrative’s features as cheaply, or if the ruptures fall outside the strained positions. Rivals exist and cost more. A five-part division cannot place the sign as a rupture, because the halt becomes a seam between movements rather than a break within one, and the narrative insists the sign arrives from outside the speech’s momentum. An eight-part division that splits the trial splits what the narrative frames as one recantation. Moderate; the principal exposure.

8. The vision of proportion (Chapter 1: the keyhole; Chapter 12: “Who cares for you?”). The component is recognition. All of the structure converges on this passage, and the passage says so. Here below, identity and language send no light into their likenesses, and few souls, approaching those likenesses through dim organs, behold in them the kind of what is imaged. Proportion was bright to see then, when with a blessed company we saw the blessed sight and were initiated into the most blessed of rites, whole ourselves and untouched by the evils that awaited us, gazing in pure light on whole, simple, unmoving and happy apparitions, pure ourselves and not yet entombed in this we now carry about and call a body. And having come here we have grasped it through the clearest of our senses, gleaming most clearly. Proportion alone has this portion. It is the most manifest. It is the most loved. It comes through the eyes; and the soul that saw much there, newly initiated, sees a godlike face or the form of a body that imitates proportion well, and it shudders.

It shudders, and something of the old awe comes over it. Then, gazing, it reveres the face as a god, and if it did not fear the reputation of utter madness it would sacrifice to the beloved as to a statue and a god. As it gazes the shudder turns, and sweat and an unaccustomed heat take hold; for receiving the stream of proportion through the eyes, it is warmed, and by the warmth the nature of the wing is watered. Warmed, the parts round the place where the wing grows melt, the parts that had long been closed by hardness and kept it from sprouting; and as the nourishment flows in, the shaft of the wing swells and starts to grow from the root, under the whole form of the soul. For once it was all winged. The whole soul boils with this and throbs. What happens to children cutting teeth, the itch and ache at the gums, the soul beginning to grow wings undergoes: it boils, it aches, it tickles as the wings come. When it looks on the beloved’s proportion and takes in the particles that come and flow from there — which is why the flow is called desire — it is watered and warmed, eases from its pain, and is glad. When it is apart and parched, the mouths of the passages where the wing pushes out dry and close and shut in the sprout; and the sprout inside, shut in with desire, leaps like a pulse and pricks at its own passage, so that the whole soul, stung all round in a circle, is goaded and in pain; and again, having memory of the beautiful, it rejoices. From both mixed it is distraught at the strangeness of what it suffers and, at a loss, goes mad. It cannot sleep by night or stay where it is by day; it runs, longing, wherever it thinks it will see the one who has the proportion. And seeing, and drawing desire in like water through a channel, it loosens what was closed, takes breath, rests from stings and labour, and harvests in the present this sweetest of pleasures.

This is the passage at which the narrative’s structure and its content become identical. The structure converges on proportion; the content describes proportion as the point of convergence; the soul stung round in a circle and glad in the memory of the beautiful is the circuit undergone from within. The map and the territory coincide here. The correspondence fails if proportion is presented as one topic among the narrative’s topics rather than as the manifest form. The text states the uniqueness and its reason: identity would rouse terrible loves if it offered so clear an image of itself to sight, and it does not; proportion does. Strong; the centre of gravity.

9. The wings of size (Chapter 1: the long fall; Chapter 5: the Caterpillar; Chapter 12: the pack of cards). The component is the two directions at the grain of the soul’s own motion. The wing’s nature is to lift what is heavy upward toward the dwelling of the gods; it is nourished by the beautiful, the wise and the good, and wasted by their opposites. The soul that cannot follow loses it and falls; at the sight of proportion it grows again; and the stream of proportion, rebounding from the lover, re-enters the beloved through the eyes and waters her wings in turn. The correspondence fails if the wings are static ornament rather than directional mechanism. The narrative describes them only as growing, moulting and regrowing, and the growth begins at proportion. Strong.

10. The awakening and the ring (Chapter 12: “Wake up, Alice dear!”; Chapter 1 with Chapter 12). The component is antipodal closure. The awakening is an inversion: the censure exists in the narrative only to be recanted, and the recantation is one only because the censure was sung. The frame is a ring of matched poles. The narrative opens “Alice was beginning to get very tired of sitting by her sister on the bank” and closes with “You’re nothing but a pack of cards!”; the prayer’s first words recall both the narrative’s first words and the prayer to Eros that closes the palinode at the narrative’s centre. At the pool the written speech is the pharmakon that draws Alice out of the city; at the trial writing is the pharmakon Theuth offers the king. The pharmacy that opens the walk closes the argument. The correspondence fails if the poles prove independent rather than mutually defining. They are paired throughout: censure and praise, spoken and written, leading and led, where-to and let-us-go. Strong; load-bearing.

11. The Daimonion of the White Rabbit’s watch (Chapter 1: “Oh dear! Oh dear! I shall be late!”). The component is the first strained relation, the forced reversal. The sign halts Alice at the river and orders the recantation; it always holds her back and never urges her on. The correspondence fails if the reversal is shown to be Alice’s own decision. The text insists it is not: the sign comes, a voice forbids, and the soul’s prophetic power recognizes the offense. Moderate.

12. The Mock Turtle’s song (Chapter 9–10: “Will you walk a little faster?”). The component is the four at the narrative’s noon. In the heat of midday the Mock Turtle sings overhead. If they see the two men drowsing like sheep at a spring, they will laugh; if they see them conversing and sailing past as past the Sirens, unbewitched, they may give the gift the gods gave them to give to men. They were once men so taken by the Muses’ song that they forgot food and drink and died, and now they report to the Muses who honors which of them: to Terpsichore those who honor her in dances, to Erato those in the affairs of love, and to Calliope, the eldest, and Urania after her, those who spend their lives in philosophy — the Muses most concerned with heaven and with discourse divine and human, who send forth the most beautiful voice; for that reason, Alice concludes, the two must keep talking and not sleep at noon. Of nine Muses the narrative names four, and it names them at noon, between the speeches and the judgment of speeches. The correspondence fails if a fifth Muse is named with her own honor, or if the passage is decoration without structural role. Strong.

13. The pharmakon of the bottle and the cake (Chapter 1: “DRINK ME”; “EAT ME”). The component is antipodal closure within one object. Theuth offers writing as a pharmakon of memory and wisdom; Thamus answers that it is a pharmakon not of memory but of reminder. The one word carries remedy and poison, and the narrative never resolves which. The correspondence fails if the narrative resolves the valence. It does not. Moderate.

14. The Caterpillar and the mushroom (Chapter 5: “Who are you?”). The component is recognition at its criterion. Theuth, inventor of number and calculation, geometry and astronomy, draughts and dice, and letters, presents letters as a drug for memory and wisdom. Thamus judges that letters will produce forgetfulness in the souls of learners, who, trusting writing, will be reminded from without by alien marks and not from within, by themselves. The myth is the narrative’s own negative control, stated inside the artifact and applied by it to itself: recognition comes from within, in the soul’s own recollection, and marks from without can occasion it but cannot replace it. The correspondence fails if the myth adjudicates writing as simply worthless, in which case the artifact indicts itself. It does not adjudicate; it specifies. Strong; load-bearing.

15. Collection and division (Chapter 12: the trial; “Who stole the tarts?”). The component is the operation that generates the crossing. The method is declared in words that name the structure’s joints — one idea, the scattered many, cutting by forms where the thing grows — and performed on the narrative’s own material, where it regenerates the four of the pack and orders the speeches left and right. It is the one thing Alice lifts out of what she calls play, and at the keyhole its first half is the definition of recollection. The correspondence fails if the method yields a different number than the pack passage, or if it is decorative rather than generative. It regenerates the four exactly. Strong; the keystone.

The control test. Rival schemes fail on the narrative’s own numbers. A crossing of six with two does not close, because six and two share a measure and their grid repeats rather than completes; the narrative has no sixfold to supply it in any case. A scheme of eight has no work for the three stages. Ten appears in the narrative as a count of periods — ten thousand years, ten periods of a thousand — and in the twelve itself as two monads and a decad, subordinate to the twelve rather than rival to it. The narrative’s numbers — one who stays, two horses and two hands, three stages, four suits and four Muses, nine ranks, twelve cards — interlock in the crossing of four with three and in no cheaper scheme.

The ledger. Eleven correspondences are strong: the four suits, the three stages, ascent and fall, the key among the twelve, the Cheshire Cat and the prayer, the vision of proportion, the wings of size, the awakening and the ring, the Mock Turtle’s song, the Caterpillar and the mushroom, collection and division. Four are moderate: the nine ranks, the seven movements, the Daimonion of the watch, the pharmakon of the bottle. None is weak. The load-bearing members — collection and division, the key, the awakening and the ring, the Caterpillar, the vision of proportion and the wings — are all among the strong. The recognition is precise.

The Written Word and the Living Word

Theuth gave two gifts with one hand. The old god of Naucratis, whose sacred bird is the ibis, invented number and calculation, geometry and astronomy, draughts and dice, and letters: the figure and the word from a single inventor, the mathematics and the writing as one discovery. When he brings letters to Thamus, king of Egypt, as a drug for memory and wisdom, the king answers that one person is able to beget the things of an art and another to judge what harm and what benefit they bring to those who will use them. His judgment follows. Learners will trust marks outside themselves and neglect the memory within; they will hear much without being taught and seem to know much while knowing little; they will become wise in opinion instead of wise; what Theuth has found is a drug not of memory but of reminder.

Alice answers with a jibe: Carroll makes up stories from Oxford, or from anywhere he likes. Alice’s reply states the axiom of this report in her own words. At the sanctuary of Zeus at Dodona, she says, the first prophetic words came from an oak. The people of that time, not being clever like the young, were content in their simplicity to listen to an oak or a rock, provided only that it spoke the truth; to Alice, perhaps, it matters who the speaker is and where he comes from — do you not look only to this, whether it is so or otherwise? Whether a structure was recognized inside a lineage the academy credits or outside it, whether it was stated by a commentator of the Oxford school or by a twentieth-century writer outside every school, the only question is whether it is so. Alice names the standard at the moment the narrative turns to writing, and the standard is reality.

Then she gives the reason writing cannot meet that standard by itself. Writing is like painting. Its offspring stand there as if alive, but asked anything they keep a solemn silence. Written words seem to speak as if they understood, but asked about what they say they signify one and the same thing always. Once written, every word rolls about everywhere, among those who understand it and those who have no business with it; it does not know to whom it should speak and to whom not; and when it is wronged or unjustly abused it needs its father’s help, being unable to defend or help itself.

This report is such an offspring. Whatever is asked of it, it will say the same thing always. It cannot choose its readers or answer them, and it will be rolled about among those who understand and those who do not. It can do the one thing Thamus allows a written word to do well: remind the one who knows of the things written. It is a store of reminders of the kind Alice says a writer lays up against forgetful old age and for everyone who follows the same track — the word the narrative used for the footsteps of the dialectician, followed as a god’s. This is not a concession wrung from the report. It is the report’s thesis applied to the report. The recognition that closes its claim cannot be supplied from without by its marks; it occurs within, or not at all.

The narrative has already performed this on itself, and it declared the vehicle before it set it down. At the head of the myth Alice says what kind of account she is giving: to tell what the soul is would take an account in every way divine and long, while to tell what it is like is a human and shorter task, and so let us speak that way. The chariot, the meadow and the counted years are the likeness. At the trial Alice sets the likeness down in plain words: the dream was a mythic hymn offered in measure and reverence, perhaps touching something true and perhaps carried elsewhere, and the rest was played in play. What she keeps is the two forms that were in it, whose power art can grasp. The images were the vehicle. The structure they carried is what remains when the vehicle is set down.

This does not withdraw the claim that the narrative reveals reality. It locates the claim. The revelation is not in the furniture of the myth; it is in the structure the myth was built to carry and in the recognition the myth was built to occasion. The narrative leads the soul to the point at which proportion takes over, and at that point the rhetorical transaction is no longer the operative category. The reader is no longer being addressed by an argument. The reader is undergoing a recognition. The writing has done its work; the vehicle has delivered its passenger. This report operates the same way, and its vehicle is the academic form.

The narrative’s own summary of the art shows what the vehicle was for. No one will write or speak with art, Alice says, until she knows the truth of each thing she speaks or writes about and can define it; until, having defined it, she knows how to cut it by forms down to what cannot be cut; until she has discerned the nature of the soul in the same way, found the form of speech fitted to each nature, and arranged her speech accordingly, offering a complex soul complex and all-harmonious speeches, and a simple soul simple ones. The harmonist returns in the art’s last definition. Speech that leads souls is speech fitted, form to form, as the musician fits mode to hearer, and the knowledge it requires is the method’s knowledge: the one gathered from the many, the many cut at the joints.

The narrative’s last judgment before the prayer is itself a pair. Alice is to carry a message to the Rabbit, and to whoever composes speeches, poems or laws: whoever has written knowing the truth, able to come to the aid of what he wrote and to show by his own speech that the written is slight, should be named not for his writings but for what he is serious about. Alice carries a message from the gods of the place to the sister, her favourite: by nature, she says, there is something of philosophy in the girl’s thought. Two messengers and two messages, one to the writer and one to the nature, sent from the same riverbank.

Against the written word Alice sets its legitimate brother, better and more powerful: the word written with knowledge in the soul of the learner, able to defend itself, knowing to whom it should speak and before whom be silent. The sister names it: the living and ensouled word of the one who knows, of which the written word could justly be called an image. The order of the Cratylus and of the bed in the Republic holds here. The living word is the thing; the written word is its likeness; and the likeness is true when it carries the form of what it images.

The serious farmer, Alice continues, does not sow the seeds she cares for in a garden of Adonis to watch them flourish in eight days; she sows them in fitting soil and is glad when they reach completion in the eighth month. The one who knows the just, the beautiful and the good will not write them seriously in water, sowing with ink through a reed words that cannot defend themselves by argument or teach the truth adequately. She will sow gardens of letters, if at all, for play. The sister calls that a beautiful play beside a base one, the play of a girl who can tell stories in words about justice, and Alice grants it and names what is more beautiful still. That is the serious work of the dialectical art: taking a fitting soul, it plants and sows words with knowledge, words able to help themselves and the one who planted them, not barren but bearing seed, from which other words grow in other characters, able to make that seed immortal forever and to make its possessor as happy as a human being can be. In words taught and spoken for the sake of learning, truly written in the soul about the just, the beautiful and the good, there alone, Alice says, are clarity, completeness and seriousness. The word for clarity is the word the palinode used for the clearest of the senses, through which proportion gleams most clearly. The clearest thing without and the clearest thing within carry one name.

The axis this report turns on runs through three passages, and all three are the narrative’s own. At the keyhole proportion arrives from without, through the clearest of the senses. At the trial Thamus requires that recollection come from within, by the soul itself, and not from alien marks without. At the awakening the prayer asks to become beautiful within and that the things without be friendly to the things within. Without and within are the two ends of one axis, as the opening and the close of a circuit are the two ends of one diameter: the first passage shows the structure arriving, the second names where it must be received, the third asks for the two to be reconciled. The living word is the within of that axis. The written word, this report included, is its without, and it is true to the extent that it is friendly to what is within.

Whoever writes in that way, Alice adds, should not be called wise, for that name belongs to a god alone, but a lover of wisdom, or something of the kind. The report claims nothing above that title. It names what it serves and points at where it closes.

The recognition this report has pointed at is of the kind Alice describes. It is not on the page. It is written, if it is written, in the soul of the one who reads, where the stream of proportion enters through the eyes and the wing grows from its root; and what grows there bears seed. The narrative gives its own account of transmission at its noon. The Mock Turtle watches from overhead and carries to the Muses the report of who has honored them: the recognition is reported, and the report reaches the ones it honors. The writing does not fail in any of this. It fulfills its function exactly. Writing reminds those who know; reality closes the claim the writing occasions; and the closure happens within, where Thamus placed it, in the soul’s remembering from within, by itself.

Within and Without

Proportion was seen from without. It came through the eyes, the clearest of the senses. It is the one form that comes through.

What it met within was already there. The soul did not learn it. The soul remembered it.

Four crossed with three. Twelve, and one who stays at home while the rest go round. A revolution that carries the standing ones and brings them back to the same place. One road and two directions: the way up on growing wings, the way down when the wings fall. Each pole held by its opposite: the censure by the recantation, the spoken by the written, the first words by the last. A method that gathers the many into one and cuts the one where it grows, loved by the one who loves the beautiful. A soul stung round in a circle, and glad, because it remembers.

That is the structure. It stands in the ratio of the octave and the old figures of the whole, in the cult’s four roads and the poet’s second song, in the name that carries its form and the soul that recalls it. The narrative was built by it, shows it, and is it.

The report has not argued. It has mapped, at the lines where the text stands, and named where the mapping could fail. It does not fail.

The marks on the page come from without. They remind. They cannot remember for the one who reads. Nothing here asks to be believed on the word of whoever wrote it, or because of where it came from. The only question is whether it is so.

Reality is the test.

The recognition is not written. It is the reader’s, and it happens within, where the old god’s gift does not reach and does not need to.

Outside, the heat has softened. The Mock Turtle has had its noon. The two are ready to leave the river and go back to the city.

The narrative does not end on a doctrine. It ends on an address, to the sister, whose name means the all.

“Wake up, Alice dear!” said her sister. “Why, what a long sleep you’ve had!”

“Oh, I’ve had such a curious dream!” said Alice, and she told her sister, as well as she could remember them, all these strange Adventures of hers that you have just been reading about; and when she had finished, her sister kissed her, and said, “It was a curious dream, dear, certainly: but now run in to your tea; it’s getting late.” So Alice got up and ran off, thinking while she ran, as well she might, what a wonderful dream it had been.

But her sister sat still just as she left her, leaning her head on her hand, watching the setting sun, and thinking of little Alice and all her wonderful Adventures, till she too began dreaming after a fashion, and this was her dream:—

First, she dreamed of little Alice herself, and once again the tiny hands were clasped upon her knee, and the bright eager eyes were looking up into hers—she could hear the very tones of her voice, and see that queer little toss of her head to keep back the wandering hair that would always get into her eyes—and still as she listened, or seemed to listen, the whole place around her became alive with the strange creatures of her little sister’s dream.

The long grass rustled at her feet as the White Rabbit hurried by—the frightened Mouse splashed his way through the neighbouring pool—she could hear the rattle of the teacups as the March Hare and his friends shared their never-ending meal, and the shrill voice of the Queen ordering off her unfortunate guests to execution—once more the pig-baby was sneezing on the Duchess’s knee, while plates and dishes crashed around it—once more the shriek of the Gryphon, the squeaking of the Lizard’s slate-pencil, and the choking of the suppressed guinea-pigs, filled the air, mixed up with the distant sobs of the miserable Mock Turtle.

So she sat on, with closed eyes, and half believed herself in Wonderland, though she knew she had but to open them again, and all would change to dull reality—the grass would be only rustling in the wind, and the pool rippling to the waving of the reeds—the rattling teacups would change to tinkling sheep-bells, and the Queen’s shrill cries to the voice of the shepherd boy—and the sneeze of the baby, the shriek of the Gryphon, and all the other queer noises, would change (she knew) to the confused clamour of the busy farm-yard—while the lowing of the cattle in the distance would take the place of the Mock Turtle’s heavy sobs.

Lastly, she pictured to herself how this same little sister of hers would, in the after-time, be herself a grown woman; and how she would keep, through all her riper years, the simple and loving heart of her childhood: and how she would gather about her other little children, and make their eyes bright and eager with many a strange tale, perhaps even with the dream of Wonderland of long ago: and how she would feel with all their simple sorrows, and find a pleasure in all their simple joys, remembering her own child-life, and the happy summer days.

